WEBVTT

00:14.000 --> 00:15.400
[wind blowing]

00:27.880 --> 00:29.440
[wind howling]

00:34.560 --> 00:36.120
[phone ringing in distance]

00:40.480 --> 00:42.480
[theme song playing]

00:57.400 --> 01:00.600
♪ Take a little walk to the edge of town ♪

01:00.680 --> 01:03.240
♪ Go across the tracks ♪

01:05.080 --> 01:09.200
♪ Where the viaduct looms
Like a bird of doom ♪

01:09.280 --> 01:12.560
♪ As it shifts and cracks ♪

01:13.560 --> 01:18.200
♪ Where secrets lie in the border fires
In the humming wires ♪

01:18.280 --> 01:19.440
[phone continues ringing]

01:19.520 --> 01:22.560
♪ Hey, man, you know
You're never coming back ♪

01:23.240 --> 01:24.280
Arthur.

01:24.840 --> 01:28.120
[Arthur] "We cannot give you the assurance
you are asking for.

01:29.320 --> 01:32.120
We expect payment in full."

01:33.680 --> 01:35.440
Here's my favourite part.

01:37.240 --> 01:40.840
"We have never even heard of your people,

01:41.600 --> 01:45.240
so we are not afraid of your threats."

01:45.880 --> 01:50.560
And they've signed it,
"The Angels of Retribution."

01:53.600 --> 01:54.520
Hmm.

01:54.600 --> 01:57.920
♪ He'll rekindle all the dreams ♪

01:58.000 --> 02:02.560
♪ It took you a lifetime to destroy ♪

02:03.520 --> 02:06.240
♪ He'll reach deep into the hole ♪

02:06.320 --> 02:08.520
♪ Heal your shrinking soul ♪

02:08.600 --> 02:12.360
♪ But there won't be a single thing
That you can do ♪

02:12.440 --> 02:16.039
♪ He's a God, he's a man
He's a ghost, he's a guru ♪

02:16.120 --> 02:17.000
[coins clatter]

02:19.720 --> 02:23.200
♪ They're whispering his name
Through this disappearing land... ♪

02:23.280 --> 02:25.280
They say they haven't even heard of us.

02:25.360 --> 02:28.480
So tell Aberama and Isiah
to introduce themselves.

02:29.120 --> 02:31.440
And Finn, you stay out of it.

02:36.520 --> 02:39.720
♪ You don't have no money? ♪

02:39.800 --> 02:41.400
♪ He'll get you some ♪

02:44.720 --> 02:46.960
♪ You don't have no car? ♪

02:48.080 --> 02:50.080
♪ He'll get you one ♪

02:53.360 --> 02:58.000
♪ You don't have no self-respect
You feel like an insect ♪

02:58.080 --> 03:02.320
♪ Well, don't you worry, buddy
'Cause here he comes ♪

03:02.400 --> 03:07.760
♪ Through the ghettos and the barrio
And the Bowery and the slum ♪

03:09.640 --> 03:14.160
♪ A shadow is cast wherever he stands ♪

03:14.240 --> 03:18.400
♪ Stacks of green paper
In his red right hand ♪

03:34.200 --> 03:35.760
[phone ringing]

03:45.400 --> 03:46.720
[phone continues ringing]

03:49.280 --> 03:50.120
[groans]

03:52.720 --> 03:54.200
[breathing heavily]

04:08.800 --> 04:09.800
Yeah?

04:13.880 --> 04:16.720
Wait. Wait, wait, say that again.

04:19.440 --> 04:20.320
No.

04:20.960 --> 04:22.440
No, that cannot be possible!

04:22.519 --> 04:23.640
[machine clicking]

04:29.480 --> 04:30.360
[breathing heavily]

04:33.280 --> 04:34.320
[gasping]

04:39.680 --> 04:40.520
[moaning]

04:47.360 --> 04:51.160
Peter, my beautiful pilot.

04:55.600 --> 05:02.040
Now, will you please fly me
and my winnings back to England?

05:13.120 --> 05:15.360
Ten spoonfuls of sugar for me, please.

05:16.640 --> 05:18.120
Is that for one?

05:18.200 --> 05:19.240
Look, Ruby.

05:19.880 --> 05:21.520
Daddy's back from his call.

05:23.840 --> 05:26.080
Everything's going to be all right now.

05:54.600 --> 05:56.120
[children shouting]

05:56.200 --> 05:57.080
[tyres screech]

06:15.120 --> 06:15.960
[door bangs open]

06:25.080 --> 06:26.080
[machine clicking]

06:26.160 --> 06:27.200
[Michael] No.

06:30.640 --> 06:32.120
Oh, no, no.

06:32.800 --> 06:34.640
No, no, no.

06:45.720 --> 06:47.160
Oh, no.

06:47.240 --> 06:48.080
[snorts]

06:49.240 --> 06:50.080
[sighs]

06:52.360 --> 06:53.760
[phone rings]

06:53.840 --> 06:54.680
Hello.

06:54.760 --> 06:56.520
Arthur! Arthur, where's Tommy?

07:03.320 --> 07:05.480
Just get to him
and tell him that we've lost the lot.

07:05.560 --> 07:07.600
Stay in. Stay put.

07:07.680 --> 07:09.480
You'll hear from Tommy.

07:11.080 --> 07:13.320
Arthur, what are you talking about?
How is that possible?

07:13.400 --> 07:17.840
[Arthur] It's possible because this
morning in New York City at 6:00 a.m.,

07:17.920 --> 07:21.440
the Wall Street stock exchange crashed.

07:21.520 --> 07:23.360
Like a steam train.

07:23.440 --> 07:25.440
And we were most definitely on board.

07:27.280 --> 07:28.440
[woman speaking Chinese]

07:28.520 --> 07:29.440
[people screaming]

07:32.880 --> 07:35.600
[breathing deeply] Fuck it!

07:35.680 --> 07:36.760
[women speaking Chinese]

07:49.240 --> 07:50.640
All right. Come on, get changed.

07:50.720 --> 07:52.800
-Jesus Christ!
-Bring the car round.

07:53.440 --> 07:54.280
[woman screaming]

08:01.080 --> 08:03.280
Got to get out of here.
Hurry up! Come on!

08:03.360 --> 08:04.760
I'm going!

08:05.600 --> 08:06.760
Change of plan.

08:07.720 --> 08:10.240
We're not going to London anymore.
We're going straight to Birmingham.

08:10.320 --> 08:12.680
-There are other passengers.
-Not anymore.

08:14.360 --> 08:15.520
[baby crying]

08:32.120 --> 08:33.480
[women speaking Chinese]

08:35.040 --> 08:36.160
[lift bell chimes]

08:41.000 --> 08:42.000
If you're going to England,

08:42.080 --> 08:43.159
-I'm coming with you.
-No, no, no.

08:43.240 --> 08:45.799
I love you, you love me. That's the truth.

08:45.880 --> 08:47.640
Gina, wait, come on.

08:47.720 --> 08:49.080
I wanna meet your family.

08:49.159 --> 08:50.120
No, you don't.

08:58.640 --> 08:59.480
[gunshot]

09:05.200 --> 09:06.200
-[gunshot]
-[groans]

09:10.720 --> 09:11.680
Oh, fuck.

09:20.040 --> 09:21.280
[groans]

09:34.240 --> 09:36.120
Now you've heard of us.

09:45.160 --> 09:47.040
-[Finn grunting]
-[Aberama] Come on.

09:47.680 --> 09:49.440
-[breathing heavily]
-Couch. Couch!

09:49.520 --> 09:52.560
Put something down for the blood.
My sister'll fucking kill me.

09:53.040 --> 09:54.440
Be fucking careful.

09:54.520 --> 09:56.840
She spends thousands of pounds
on this shit.

09:56.920 --> 09:58.360
[grunts and groans]

09:58.440 --> 09:59.640
[Aberama] Get me some booze.

09:59.720 --> 10:00.560
[yelps]

10:04.040 --> 10:05.680
[screams and groans]

10:05.760 --> 10:09.160
Just fucking get the thing out of me
and sew me up. Just do it.

10:12.560 --> 10:14.000
Peaky boy, give me your blade.

10:20.760 --> 10:22.040
That's enough.

10:22.120 --> 10:24.080
Don't want you throwing up
on your sister's furniture.

10:24.840 --> 10:25.680
[whimpers]

10:26.320 --> 10:27.440
You know what you're doing?

10:27.520 --> 10:29.560
-[Aberama] I've done this a dozen times.
-[Finn breathing heavily]

10:29.640 --> 10:33.360
I once took a bullet from between
two ribs, one inch from the heart.

10:33.440 --> 10:35.440
Mind you, it was a horse. [chuckles]

10:35.520 --> 10:36.520
And the horse did die.

10:36.600 --> 10:38.240
-[both chuckle]
-Hold him. Hold him.

10:39.280 --> 10:40.520
[Finn screams]

10:41.160 --> 10:42.560
[groaning and screaming]

10:46.720 --> 10:47.560
[gasping]

10:51.160 --> 10:52.800
-Here you go.
-[chuckles]

10:52.880 --> 10:53.920
Just a little one.

10:55.840 --> 10:57.760
-[Ada] What the fuck is going on?
-Oh, fuck.

10:59.160 --> 11:00.000
Hello, Ada.

11:00.520 --> 11:03.440
-Get out. Both of you.
-[Finn] Oh, fuck.

11:10.920 --> 11:13.000
Push this on the wound.
Keep up the pressure.

11:13.080 --> 11:14.080
Get out.

11:15.320 --> 11:16.320
I'm so sorry, Ada.

11:16.400 --> 11:18.960
They broke a statue of some thin woman.

11:19.040 --> 11:20.640
Fucking statues.

11:21.200 --> 11:22.560
What has Tommy told you?

11:23.080 --> 11:25.240
Shelbys stay out of the sporting stuff.

11:25.320 --> 11:27.960
What? To maintain your fucking reputation?

11:28.040 --> 11:28.920
Oi!

11:29.000 --> 11:30.480
You listen to me.

11:30.560 --> 11:34.280
We've got a chance.
Tommy has given us that chance.

11:34.360 --> 11:38.120
He's got power, we've got money,
and our past is left behind us.

11:38.680 --> 11:42.160
And you've got a precious young life,
Finn, you fucking idiot!

11:42.240 --> 11:44.120
Three inches to the left
and that would've been gone.

11:44.200 --> 11:45.040
[groans]

11:47.960 --> 11:50.400
You've got nothing to prove, Finn.

11:50.480 --> 11:53.440
You have got nothing to prove.

12:00.840 --> 12:03.120
[Ruby] Daddy! Daddy!

12:03.200 --> 12:06.160
Come here, you.
Come here, come here, come here. Ooh!

12:07.040 --> 12:09.280
Now, what have you been up to, eh?

12:09.880 --> 12:11.200
Any time, Johnny.

12:14.600 --> 12:16.720
All right, you heard the man. Let's go.

12:25.760 --> 12:26.880
Where's Charles?

12:26.960 --> 12:29.040
He's in the wagon. He won't come out.

12:29.600 --> 12:33.760
He heard Johnny talking Rokka.
He understands more Gypsy than we think.

12:33.840 --> 12:36.760
He heard him say you shot the horse.
I told him it was mercy.

12:36.840 --> 12:39.200
I said it's what you do
when a horse gets sick.

12:39.280 --> 12:40.640
He doesn't understand.

12:42.280 --> 12:44.960
Dad, why did you shoot him?

12:49.000 --> 12:52.440
[Tommy] Charlie, get out here now.
Let's get it done.

12:54.640 --> 12:56.320
Charles, come here.

12:59.840 --> 13:00.840
Sit down.

13:05.840 --> 13:07.080
Now listen to me.

13:09.440 --> 13:11.520
Your horse was sick. He was in pain.

13:12.200 --> 13:14.320
The vet couldn't do anything.
Even Curly couldn't do anything.

13:14.400 --> 13:15.640
I put him to sleep.

13:16.480 --> 13:17.600
It's what you do.

13:17.680 --> 13:20.440
-No, it's what you do!
-[Tommy] Charlie.

13:20.520 --> 13:24.680
[Charlie] Shoot horses, shoot people.
Everybody says!

13:25.680 --> 13:26.520
[door opens]

13:28.560 --> 13:29.400
[door closes]

13:32.840 --> 13:33.680
[sighs]

13:40.880 --> 13:42.640
[lighter clicking]

13:45.520 --> 13:46.840
[coughing]

13:54.600 --> 13:55.600
Come here.

14:03.040 --> 14:05.120
Dangerous, my beautiful horse.

14:07.800 --> 14:09.120
Too wild to race.

14:10.480 --> 14:12.360
Wouldn't take the reins or the whip.

14:14.240 --> 14:15.840
Should have been a war horse.

14:17.960 --> 14:19.520
Got tired of the pasture.

14:22.280 --> 14:24.920
Couldn't stick the peace and the quiet,
gave up on life...

14:26.160 --> 14:27.520
And he's now free.

14:32.400 --> 14:33.440
In the bleak...

14:38.040 --> 14:38.880
You're crying?

14:45.560 --> 14:46.480
[Dangerous snorts]

14:52.960 --> 14:54.000
[Dangerous neighs]

15:12.840 --> 15:15.840
Tom, I'm sorry.

15:16.440 --> 15:18.320
I didn't know your boy spoke Rokka.

15:18.400 --> 15:19.520
-[car approaching]
-[horn honking]

15:19.600 --> 15:20.880
Fill it in, Johnny.

15:26.360 --> 15:27.360
Take her.

15:28.400 --> 15:30.760
I've called the house. The office.

15:31.600 --> 15:35.400
But if I tell you, you won't believe it.

15:38.080 --> 15:39.440
So I'll show you.

15:39.880 --> 15:40.720
Huh?

15:54.240 --> 15:56.920
I told Michael on Friday
this was going to happen.

15:57.000 --> 15:58.000
[Arthur] Yeah.

15:58.920 --> 16:05.720
Fucking Michael said he was advised by
the broker that the prices would rebound.

16:05.800 --> 16:08.200
Everybody was told the same thing.

16:12.400 --> 16:13.560
He held on.

16:19.880 --> 16:21.760
-Michael held on...
-Yeah.

16:30.160 --> 16:31.040
Michael.

16:38.800 --> 16:40.040
[Tommy] Michael held on.

16:41.200 --> 16:42.040
[Arthur] Yeah.

16:44.680 --> 16:46.160
Michael held on,

16:47.520 --> 16:51.000
and carried on dancing and playing

16:51.680 --> 16:53.600
and fucking in the snow.

16:55.360 --> 16:56.360
Charlie!

16:58.160 --> 16:59.800
Charlie, come out here now.

17:00.960 --> 17:02.760
The kid never fucking listens.

17:02.840 --> 17:04.520
He never listens, Lizzie.

17:06.800 --> 17:12.880
[screaming] What do I have to do
to make people fucking listen to me?

17:12.960 --> 17:14.560
Holy fuck.

17:15.680 --> 17:17.560
[Arthur] What do we do now, Tommy, eh?

17:20.680 --> 17:21.800
What do we do?

17:26.720 --> 17:30.400
Lizzie, you go with Arthur to Birmingham.

17:30.480 --> 17:31.640
You stay in the Midland Hotel.

17:31.720 --> 17:32.560
[snaps fingers]

17:32.640 --> 17:36.320
Arthur, call a full meeting for
the board of directors tomorrow at noon.

17:36.400 --> 17:37.840
A full fucking meeting.

17:37.920 --> 17:39.360
What about today, eh?

17:39.440 --> 17:41.120
What's wrong with today, Tom?

17:41.200 --> 17:42.640
[Tommy] Need to do some thinking.

17:42.720 --> 17:45.800
Oh. Oh, you do that best
on your own, don't you, eh?

17:45.880 --> 17:47.920
I won't be on my own. I'm never on my own.

17:48.840 --> 17:51.240
Lizzie, you have the driver
come back tomorrow.

17:52.760 --> 17:59.120
And Arthur, tell my boy that sometimes,
death is a kindness.

17:59.840 --> 18:00.720
Come on.

18:03.800 --> 18:05.000
[horse whinnies]

18:51.920 --> 18:52.960
[Tommy] What now?

18:54.840 --> 18:56.200
[Grace] What am I, a genie?

18:56.760 --> 18:59.000
You summon me up
with your little bottle of dope.

19:00.360 --> 19:03.800
I take it for the pain and to keep warm.

19:12.880 --> 19:14.360
Is that what it's for?

19:15.560 --> 19:16.800
The warmth?

19:18.920 --> 19:19.760
[sighs]

19:19.840 --> 19:21.000
[whispers] The warmth.

19:25.000 --> 19:26.400
All this time...

19:28.000 --> 19:29.080
[Grace] I know.

19:31.960 --> 19:34.080
Our love still remains.

19:37.480 --> 19:40.720
But you have to listen
to the voices that you hear.

19:43.600 --> 19:45.360
Do what they tell you to do.

19:47.360 --> 19:49.320
Too much to do, Grace.

19:52.040 --> 19:53.120
The kids...

19:58.240 --> 19:59.840
No need to say goodbye.

20:01.160 --> 20:02.720
[horse snorting]

20:02.800 --> 20:04.200
No need to sleep.

20:06.280 --> 20:07.760
Just think, Tom.

20:17.160 --> 20:18.040
[snorts]

20:18.880 --> 20:20.560
-[Arthur] Linda!
-[footsteps approaching]

20:20.640 --> 20:21.600
[whispers] Fuck.

20:23.280 --> 20:24.360
[door opens]

20:26.080 --> 20:26.920
Oh.

20:27.000 --> 20:28.120
[door closes]

20:28.200 --> 20:29.800
There you are, eh?

20:29.880 --> 20:30.720
[door locks]

20:33.840 --> 20:36.640
[Linda] You're the chairman, Arthur,
so start acting like one.

20:37.200 --> 20:38.640
Chairman, am I? Hmm?

20:40.760 --> 20:41.760
No.

20:43.080 --> 20:46.320
I'm the fucking doorman, Linda.

20:46.760 --> 20:47.600
Hmm?

20:48.280 --> 20:51.520
And you ain't coming in here tonight,
love, dressed like that.

20:54.880 --> 20:59.800
Tommy made me the chairman
so that he could stay clean.

21:03.120 --> 21:04.360
It's called an arrangement.

21:05.560 --> 21:07.000
It's an opportunity.

21:19.560 --> 21:22.320
I know who I am, Linda.

21:25.160 --> 21:27.760
I know who I am,
and I'm all right with it.

21:28.960 --> 21:31.000
Finally. Hmm.

21:31.760 --> 21:33.240
I can live with it.

21:46.800 --> 21:48.800
I need you to be all right with it.

21:52.960 --> 21:55.360
I need you to be all right with it.

21:55.840 --> 21:56.680
Eh?

21:57.600 --> 21:59.080
What is it, eh?

22:00.440 --> 22:01.920
And what do you want, hmm?

22:03.320 --> 22:05.520
Would you want the chairman, do you?

22:06.920 --> 22:08.880
With his fancy briefcase?

22:09.520 --> 22:11.840
And his gold fountain pen, eh?

22:11.920 --> 22:12.760
Hmm? [chuckles]

22:14.440 --> 22:15.520
What do you want?

22:17.280 --> 22:21.520
Chairman or doorman, hmm?

22:22.080 --> 22:26.320
You think we can fuck and it'll be okay,
and I'll be quiet? Well, I won't.

22:27.680 --> 22:29.120
Every week, your brother's down in London

22:29.200 --> 22:32.000
making out he's fucking Robin Hood,
champion of the people,

22:32.080 --> 22:34.240
while you're up here
running the racket for him.

22:34.840 --> 22:36.640
And according to your arrangement,
if anything should happen,

22:36.720 --> 22:40.080
it'd be you who takes the blame,
does the time, or swings for it.

22:46.160 --> 22:47.240
And what do you want?

22:47.320 --> 22:48.560
I want you...

22:49.040 --> 22:50.000
[Arthur inhales sharply]

22:50.080 --> 22:51.320
...to take the opportunity

22:51.400 --> 22:53.560
that God and the New York Stock Exchange

22:53.640 --> 22:55.000
-has presented to you.
-[footsteps approaching]

22:55.080 --> 22:56.240
[knocking at door]

22:56.320 --> 22:58.840
[Edna] Mr Shelby, the meeting
will be starting soon.

22:58.920 --> 23:00.840
Yeah, all right.

23:02.880 --> 23:04.120
Be right there.

23:34.000 --> 23:35.000
[Arthur] Thank you, Edna.

23:40.200 --> 23:41.880
And shut the door!

23:41.960 --> 23:42.800
[door closes]

23:44.240 --> 23:45.320
[Linda clears throat]

23:47.520 --> 23:49.600
Okay, now.

23:51.040 --> 23:52.600
Hmm. Very nice.

23:53.560 --> 23:54.640
Let's begin.

23:55.640 --> 23:59.680
Yesterday, as you all know,
there was a fucking terrible...

24:02.160 --> 24:03.720
There was a terrible disaster.

24:03.800 --> 24:04.640
Hmm?

24:05.440 --> 24:09.440
It wasn't just for us,
it was for the whole world.

24:11.560 --> 24:16.040
It happened in New York.
That's where it happened.

24:16.600 --> 24:18.200
And it slowly spread to London.

24:19.200 --> 24:22.760
And then to, er, fucking Hong Kong. Hmm?

24:25.840 --> 24:28.080
The whole thing...
The whole thing went up.

24:28.760 --> 24:32.520
Well, actually, the whole thing
went fucking down, but...

24:34.600 --> 24:35.440
[Arthur clears throat]

24:36.320 --> 24:39.440
We wasn't the only ones hurt by this.

24:42.200 --> 24:45.440
Whole world is hurt, a lot of people.

24:47.040 --> 24:48.440
In the papers.

24:48.520 --> 24:51.400
It's all over the news.
Everyone's talking about it.

24:51.480 --> 24:52.560
Arthur,

24:53.400 --> 24:55.000
shouldn't we just wait for Thomas?

24:55.080 --> 24:58.840
[chuckles] Polly, Arthur's now
the chairman of the board.

25:00.640 --> 25:03.400
[Greene] Er, Mr Chairman,
perhaps while we wait for Thomas,

25:03.480 --> 25:05.800
I might lighten the gloom and express,

25:05.880 --> 25:08.600
as a relatively new member
of this company,

25:08.680 --> 25:12.160
what a pleasure it is to be in a boardroom
that has so many females in it.

25:12.240 --> 25:17.360
Yeah, and all are females who are
both... sharp-witted and decorative.

25:17.440 --> 25:20.280
[Arthur] Yeah, well,
we're a very modern company, Mr Greene.

25:20.800 --> 25:21.960
Indeed.

25:22.040 --> 25:26.640
Although, there are still some amongst us
who cling to their old-fashioned values.

25:27.160 --> 25:28.000
[door opens]

25:30.600 --> 25:31.840
How far have we got?

25:32.880 --> 25:35.080
We've established
that ladies are decorative.

25:35.640 --> 25:36.960
[Arthur] We've only just sat down, Tom.

25:37.040 --> 25:41.120
Actually, erm, Arthur has some documents
that he'd like to hand out.

25:41.200 --> 25:42.120
[Tommy] Yeah?

25:42.680 --> 25:43.800
What documents?

25:45.160 --> 25:47.920
I was just explaining
to everyone, Tom, that, erm...

25:48.520 --> 25:51.000
well, we're fucked. Ain't that right, hmm?

25:51.080 --> 25:53.160
Yes and no. [clears throat]

25:54.000 --> 25:57.360
It is true that a large proportion
of the company's funds

25:58.360 --> 26:01.080
were invested in American stocks
and shares.

26:01.160 --> 26:02.320
Invested or hidden?

26:04.240 --> 26:06.480
All investments are approved
by the board, Linda.

26:06.560 --> 26:09.480
Only the ones that went
through the books, Thomas.

26:11.320 --> 26:15.600
Linda, after this meeting, there will be
another meeting, for just family.

26:16.280 --> 26:17.520
Did we vote on this?

26:22.640 --> 26:23.480
Arthur--

26:23.560 --> 26:25.200
[mocking] "Arthur,
can't you control your wife?"

26:26.200 --> 26:28.400
We are a very modern company, Mr Greene.

26:31.560 --> 26:32.400
[Tommy clears throat]

26:32.480 --> 26:37.400
To be precise, everything we channelled
through the New York Stock Exchange

26:37.480 --> 26:41.240
is now offering a return
of ten cents to the dollar.

26:41.800 --> 26:42.640
[scoffs]

26:42.720 --> 26:46.360
And the Nolan Bank of America,
where we invested our fluidity,

26:46.440 --> 26:50.280
is offering five cents to the dollar
on all deposits.

26:51.120 --> 26:52.000
Yeah.

26:52.080 --> 26:52.920
[sighs]

26:53.480 --> 26:54.520
But there is hope.

26:54.600 --> 26:57.600
Why? What have you got in there,
a magic wand?

27:01.680 --> 27:03.520
As non-executive director of the company,

27:03.600 --> 27:07.920
I need permission from the chairman
to present my strategy to the board.

27:08.840 --> 27:10.360
Permission granted, Tom.

27:10.440 --> 27:11.480
[scoffs]

27:27.280 --> 27:30.800
♪ Misty morning, clouds in the sky ♪

27:32.120 --> 27:35.920
♪ Without warning, a wizard walks by ♪

27:36.920 --> 27:40.760
♪ Casting his shadow, weaving his spell ♪

27:41.720 --> 27:45.480
♪ Funny clothes, twinkling bell ♪

27:47.200 --> 27:51.000
♪ Never talking
Just keeps walking ♪

27:52.040 --> 27:53.760
♪ Causing his magic ♪

27:56.880 --> 28:00.440
The Peaky Blinders are coming.
The Peaky Blinders are coming.

28:00.520 --> 28:02.680
Micky, the Peaky Blinders are coming.

28:05.680 --> 28:08.920
[Micky] Ladies and gents. Peaky Blinders
are on the way. Please vacate. Thank you.

28:09.000 --> 28:09.960
[indistinct chatter]

28:14.000 --> 28:15.280
[customers cheering and clapping]

28:22.040 --> 28:24.440
-[Tommy] Good to see you.
-[man 1] Mr Shelby.

28:24.520 --> 28:25.800
-[man 2] Mr Shelby.
-Yeah.

28:25.880 --> 28:27.360
Thank you for what you did.

28:27.440 --> 28:28.520
What did I do?

28:28.600 --> 28:31.240
He was gonna evict us,
that bastard, Fellows.

28:31.320 --> 28:33.640
Now he's cut our rent in half. [chuckles]

28:33.720 --> 28:35.640
Good. Glad to hear it. Glad to hear it.

28:35.720 --> 28:38.040
[Arthur] That was a strong lesson for him.

28:38.760 --> 28:41.560
How far can we go, Mr Shelby,
with this beautiful dream?

28:41.640 --> 28:43.240
All the way, brother. All the way.

28:43.320 --> 28:44.840
-Revolution, man.
-[crowd] Yeah.

28:44.920 --> 28:48.680
This crash, Mr Shelby, they're saying
they're gonna cut my hours.

28:48.760 --> 28:51.000
-Yeah? Where do you work?
-Greenhams Engineering.

28:51.080 --> 28:54.040
Greenham Brothers, I know them.
They're not gonna cut your hours.

28:54.120 --> 28:55.840
God bless you. God bless you both.

28:55.920 --> 28:57.000
[Arthur] Enjoy your drink.

28:57.080 --> 29:00.520
[Tommy] Gentlemen and ladies,
if you could all move into the Saloon bar,

29:00.600 --> 29:03.200
where you will all be served
a free pint of Stingo.

29:03.280 --> 29:04.520
[crowd cheers]

29:04.600 --> 29:07.360
[Tommy] Any man who served in France
gets a brandy chaser.

29:07.440 --> 29:08.440
Yeah!

29:08.520 --> 29:10.320
[Tommy] Despite what you're reading
in the newspapers,

29:10.400 --> 29:12.440
I want to hear singing and laughing.

29:12.520 --> 29:13.960
Fuck the stock market!

29:14.040 --> 29:15.400
[crowd] Fuck the stock market!

29:15.480 --> 29:18.640
They backed the wrong horses.
So fucking what, eh?

29:20.200 --> 29:23.120
Thank you, Mr Shelby.
A politician who gets things done.

29:23.200 --> 29:24.640
[Tommy] You're welcome. You're welcome.

29:24.720 --> 29:25.920
[Ada] Get down off the furniture.

29:26.000 --> 29:28.360
[Arthur] Sorry, Ada.
I get excited, don't I?

29:29.320 --> 29:30.160
[door closes]

29:32.760 --> 29:33.720
Right...

29:35.640 --> 29:37.720
Can I begin this family meeting
with a proposal?

29:37.800 --> 29:39.920
From now on,
we find somewhere else to meet?

29:40.000 --> 29:42.760
Your husband believes that being seen
mixing with the common people

29:42.840 --> 29:44.240
is good politics.

29:44.320 --> 29:46.600
Hmm. Well, if this is our campaign
for socialism,

29:46.680 --> 29:49.920
perhaps next time, Polly, you won't wear
earrings worth more than the pub.

29:50.000 --> 29:52.680
Right. Family meeting. First item.

29:53.480 --> 29:54.320
[Ada] This.

29:55.680 --> 29:57.600
Dug out of our Finn's arm yesterday

29:57.680 --> 30:00.120
by Aberama Gold
using your gin and a razor blade.

30:00.960 --> 30:01.800
Finn?

30:01.880 --> 30:04.960
He says they were sent
to Limehouse. Chinatown.

30:05.040 --> 30:06.440
Sent by fucking who?

30:06.520 --> 30:08.040
Sent by me. [clears throat]

30:08.120 --> 30:11.000
I told Finn to stay out of it.
He obviously didn't listen.

30:11.080 --> 30:12.160
[scoffs]

30:12.240 --> 30:15.240
Oh, Tommy, sweetheart, I listen to you.

30:15.320 --> 30:19.280
I listen to you when you tell me
no more sport for anyone named Shelby.

30:19.360 --> 30:22.840
I listen to you when you make me promises.

30:24.520 --> 30:27.200
What's in Chinatown, Tommy?
What the fuck is going on?

30:28.360 --> 30:29.200
[sighs]

30:29.280 --> 30:31.880
Fifty thousand pound. In cash.

30:31.960 --> 30:34.720
He said they were sent
to Chinatown to kill somebody.

30:34.800 --> 30:35.640
[groans]

30:36.160 --> 30:39.000
That kid needs educating,
Tommy, he really does.

30:39.080 --> 30:40.320
Jesus Christ, Tommy!

30:40.400 --> 30:42.120
It was a particular opportunity.

30:42.200 --> 30:44.360
A particular opportunity
presented to me in confidence.

30:44.440 --> 30:46.320
I dealt with it in confidence.

30:46.400 --> 30:47.760
But you told Polly.

30:48.480 --> 30:51.840
Tommy told me that there was a pimp
in East London who sold kids.

30:51.920 --> 30:54.880
Fucking kids, Lizzie, eh? Hmm?

30:55.400 --> 30:59.440
This pimp was blackmailing a senior member
of the House of Lords. A very wealthy man.

30:59.520 --> 31:02.440
Now this pimp is lying in a ditch.

31:02.520 --> 31:03.760
Covered in flies.

31:03.840 --> 31:05.440
World's a better place.

31:05.520 --> 31:09.760
Holy fuck. So now your business
is improving the world.

31:12.080 --> 31:13.760
[Arthur] Sometimes, Ada...

31:14.960 --> 31:17.040
killing is a kindness.

31:17.120 --> 31:17.960
Hmm?

31:21.560 --> 31:24.000
The man we did the job for
is a High Court judge.

31:24.560 --> 31:28.000
We received intelligence from
a senior police officer at Scotland Yard.

31:28.080 --> 31:31.840
I've made lots of new friends in London.
Men with influence.

31:32.480 --> 31:35.400
The police felt the same way
about this pimp as we did.

31:35.480 --> 31:36.520
He wasn't worth a trial.

31:36.600 --> 31:38.120
The coppers cleared the streets for us.

31:38.200 --> 31:40.840
This work was commissioned
by a High Court judge,

31:40.920 --> 31:43.480
by Scotland Yard
and by the House of Lords.

31:43.560 --> 31:46.360
It's a particular opportunity.
It's not to be repeated.

31:46.440 --> 31:48.440
-And it was the right thing to do.
-Fucking right.

31:48.520 --> 31:50.320
Lizzie, you need to understand.

31:50.400 --> 31:52.040
That you tell Polly, not me.

31:52.120 --> 31:54.000
You need to understand

31:54.080 --> 31:57.280
that the corridors of Westminster
are very dimly lit,

31:57.360 --> 32:00.520
and for those who make the rules,
there are no rules.

32:01.080 --> 32:03.600
We own the ropes.
Who's gonna hang us now, eh?

32:03.680 --> 32:06.520
-"We"? We own the ropes, do we?
-Lizzie?

32:06.600 --> 32:09.000
Lizzie, if Finn had listened to me,
you wouldn't have known.

32:09.080 --> 32:10.800
When we go home, I'll explain.

32:10.880 --> 32:11.720
[scoffs]

32:12.320 --> 32:14.080
[Lizzie] I can't be bothered
with this shit.

32:18.080 --> 32:18.920
[door opens]

32:19.640 --> 32:22.880
-So. Tommy Shelby, MP.
-[door closes]

32:23.560 --> 32:25.000
Business as usual.

32:26.240 --> 32:28.480
The strategy I outlined earlier

32:28.560 --> 32:31.760
to rebuild the conventional part
of this business will take time.

32:31.840 --> 32:32.880
In the meantime--

32:32.960 --> 32:34.520
Just business as fucking usual!

32:34.600 --> 32:35.600
In the meantime,

32:35.680 --> 32:40.600
it is fortunate that we have maintained
other sources of cash income.

32:40.680 --> 32:43.280
Because until the stock market recovers,
cash is king.

32:43.360 --> 32:44.960
And cash we have.

32:45.040 --> 32:46.600
No one is gonna hang you, Tommy.

32:46.680 --> 32:48.000
You're gonna hang yourself.

32:50.600 --> 32:53.000
Someone has to pay for them paintings
on your wall, Ada.

32:53.080 --> 32:54.960
Yeah, bye-bye, Ada.

32:55.040 --> 32:55.880
[door closes]

32:55.960 --> 32:57.600
You need to go easy on Ada.

32:58.600 --> 32:59.560
[Tommy] Polly.

33:00.400 --> 33:04.720
When am I ever not easy on Ada, eh?

33:09.840 --> 33:13.440
Thomas, the thing is, Ada...

33:14.080 --> 33:15.560
-She's pregnant.
-[coughing]

33:17.520 --> 33:18.640
Ada's what?

33:19.560 --> 33:23.280
These days, people tell me things
without even speaking.

33:23.360 --> 33:24.360
[Arthur] Wait a minute.

33:24.960 --> 33:26.080
Wait a minute.

33:26.680 --> 33:29.640
Ada's fucking pregnant, hmm?

33:30.200 --> 33:34.920
And Polly, you need to reassure Ada
that it's 1929, times have changed,

33:35.000 --> 33:38.360
we have a lot to do,
and no one gives a fuck who the father is.

33:38.440 --> 33:39.360
Really?

33:40.440 --> 33:42.360
I already know who the father is.

33:42.440 --> 33:43.280
[sighs]

33:43.800 --> 33:45.880
But we've had enough shocks for one day.

33:52.840 --> 33:53.760
Okay.

33:56.560 --> 33:57.400
[door opens]

33:59.880 --> 34:00.720
[door closes]

34:03.400 --> 34:07.840
All right, go on then.
How long you known about Ada, huh?

34:08.840 --> 34:11.200
I know her doctor. He calls me.

34:13.920 --> 34:15.880
Yeah, longer than me.

34:15.960 --> 34:16.960
Have a look at this.

34:24.600 --> 34:26.639
What's that funny writing on there?

34:26.719 --> 34:27.719
Chinese.

34:28.679 --> 34:30.280
Ah. Right.

34:32.320 --> 34:33.679
And what is it, hmm?

34:35.120 --> 34:36.120
Bad news?

34:37.120 --> 34:38.280
Maybe.

34:38.840 --> 34:39.840
[children chattering]

34:40.920 --> 34:43.880
-[Arthur] Angels of Retribution?
-[Tommy] Yeah.

34:43.960 --> 34:45.480
Burn the letter they sent.

34:46.000 --> 34:47.239
It's already done.

34:52.920 --> 34:54.480
Look at that, Tom.

34:56.760 --> 34:58.600
Pulled from our brother, eh?

34:59.840 --> 35:02.120
Finally took a first bullet. [chuckles]

35:02.800 --> 35:04.880
No one fucking listens to me.

35:09.400 --> 35:12.440
Here, give it here. I'll keep it for him.

35:12.520 --> 35:14.840
-I still have my first one.
-[chuckling] Yeah.

35:19.280 --> 35:20.720
[crowd singing in distance]

35:22.560 --> 35:26.680
Arthur, you can tell our comrades
they can come back in.

35:28.400 --> 35:29.840
Where you going, brother?

35:37.520 --> 35:43.040
♪ I really wanna fight for my country
Just like the old boys and Nazis ♪

35:43.120 --> 35:48.320
♪ Gotta feel it, know who's the enemy
Execution and familiarity ♪

35:48.400 --> 35:53.240
♪ Man is seeking, damn he's gotta destroy
Comprehend the race war, it's in ploy ♪

35:53.320 --> 35:56.040
♪ Gotta keep that dopamine joy ♪

35:56.560 --> 35:59.760
♪ Man, I gotta feel alive now ♪

36:04.320 --> 36:07.320
[man] The huge losses
on the British stock exchange

36:07.400 --> 36:11.520
will bite into our economy
for the foreseeable future.

36:11.600 --> 36:16.560
I therefore must ask the trade unions
to possess understanding.

36:16.640 --> 36:19.520
The need for their
flexibility and cooperation

36:19.600 --> 36:21.600
is paramount in the coming months

36:21.680 --> 36:24.680
as we attempt to rebuild
this great nation.

36:25.320 --> 36:26.320
[indistinct murmuring]

36:28.320 --> 36:30.080
[speaker] Mr Thomas Shelby.

36:31.240 --> 36:32.760
Thank you, Mr Speaker.

36:34.760 --> 36:38.560
My Right Honourable Friend,
the member for Epping,

36:38.640 --> 36:41.680
asks that following the crash,

36:41.760 --> 36:46.480
trade union members be more flexible
when carrying out their duties.

36:48.080 --> 36:51.480
What he's really asking is that
the working man carry the can.

36:51.960 --> 36:52.800
[murmuring]

36:53.320 --> 36:57.640
The grand casino in Monte Carlo
is a small and timid affair

36:57.720 --> 37:02.560
when compared to the wild games of chance
being played in London and New York

37:02.640 --> 37:06.320
by gamblers in silk gloves
and beaver hats,

37:06.400 --> 37:07.960
and when they lose their bets,

37:08.040 --> 37:10.320
turn around and ask
the shoe-shine to pay for it.

37:10.400 --> 37:11.320
[crowd laughing]

37:11.400 --> 37:15.400
Well, on behalf of the shoe-shines
and the can-carriers of South Birmingham

37:15.480 --> 37:17.400
and all across Great Britain,

37:17.480 --> 37:21.800
I would suggest that those
who so recklessly lost their fortunes

37:21.880 --> 37:23.120
on the capitalist lottery

37:23.880 --> 37:26.480
-learn to shine their own shoes...
-[crowd] Yes.

37:26.560 --> 37:28.480
-...carry their own cans...
-Yes.

37:28.560 --> 37:29.800
...and pay their own bills.

37:29.880 --> 37:31.480
-[crowd cheering and clapping]
-Yeah!

37:37.960 --> 37:39.320
[man] Er, Mr Shelby?

37:40.240 --> 37:43.080
I just wanted to say
how much I enjoyed your speech.

37:43.160 --> 37:44.680
You speak very eloquently.

37:44.760 --> 37:46.040
Thank you.

37:47.080 --> 37:50.320
My name's Mosley, by the way,
Oswald Mosley.

37:51.160 --> 37:52.200
Yeah, I know who you are.

37:52.280 --> 37:54.160
My constituency borders yours.

37:54.680 --> 37:58.320
And let me just say,
you've come to my attention.

38:01.160 --> 38:02.360
[knocking at door]

38:02.440 --> 38:03.400
Come.

38:03.480 --> 38:04.320
[door opens]

38:07.640 --> 38:08.480
[Suckerby] Ah.

38:10.840 --> 38:13.080
I have bad news, Mister...

38:16.680 --> 38:17.640
Shelby.

38:18.840 --> 38:22.080
We agreed £50,000
on completion of business.

38:22.760 --> 38:26.040
My wife and I have had a misfortune
on the London Exchange.

38:26.920 --> 38:28.280
There's £20,000 there.

38:28.920 --> 38:30.600
You'll have to wait for the rest.

38:35.360 --> 38:36.200
[sighs]

38:43.480 --> 38:44.560
Cavalry, eh?

38:45.920 --> 38:47.120
-Mmm.
-[chuckles]

38:49.000 --> 38:49.920
Yeah.

38:50.960 --> 38:53.800
I spent a long time
waiting for the cavalry, me.

38:56.120 --> 39:00.880
One time, me and my comrades
waited three days.

39:02.040 --> 39:03.400
When the cavalry finally came,

39:03.480 --> 39:06.800
an officer on the back
of a fine white horse

39:07.440 --> 39:10.000
joked that he'd been delayed

39:10.600 --> 39:11.960
playing a game of whist.

39:13.080 --> 39:17.040
So, I took out my Webley revolver
and I shot him in the head,

39:17.600 --> 39:20.360
stole his horse,
reported him for cowardice.

39:21.120 --> 39:22.480
All for a game of chance.

39:25.280 --> 39:28.800
After my people completed their task
in Chinatown,

39:28.880 --> 39:30.920
they collected all the photographs,

39:31.000 --> 39:34.560
letters and cheques
that were causing you concern.

39:34.640 --> 39:36.520
I now have them in a safe place.

39:37.120 --> 39:43.080
On Monday, you will give me
the full £50,000 in cash,

39:43.920 --> 39:46.720
or I will steal your white horse
from under you.

39:48.320 --> 39:52.680
How the fuck did a man like you
get into a position of trust

39:52.760 --> 39:54.800
in a place of power and privilege?

39:55.800 --> 39:56.880
A man like me?

40:01.760 --> 40:03.000
A man like me?

40:04.080 --> 40:05.080
Lord Suckerby,

40:06.000 --> 40:07.520
I've seen the photographs.

40:10.160 --> 40:11.200
Monday.

40:17.040 --> 40:17.880
[door opens]

40:17.960 --> 40:19.360
-Shelby!
-[door closes]

40:21.000 --> 40:22.240
[Younger] Sorry I'm late, Ada.

40:22.320 --> 40:24.760
We've been having briefings
from the Home Office.

40:25.400 --> 40:28.880
The police are concerned about the effect
of the crash in industrial areas.

40:28.960 --> 40:31.200
-[Ada] Ah.
-Everything all right?

40:31.280 --> 40:32.640
[sighing] Yeah, fine.

40:32.720 --> 40:35.360
We lost money in New York,
so I've been up late.

40:36.000 --> 40:37.360
Has your brother been busy?

40:38.280 --> 40:41.760
Er, he's had two meetings
with Mr Shapurji Saklatvala,

40:41.840 --> 40:43.760
former communist MP of Battersea.

40:43.840 --> 40:46.520
He's been approached
by an anarchist group in Walsall,

40:46.600 --> 40:47.960
he's setting up a meeting.

40:48.040 --> 40:50.160
And there's talk of a mass walkout
of bus drivers,

40:50.240 --> 40:53.920
Nottingham and Derby,
organised by two communist conveners,

40:54.000 --> 40:56.760
not formally known to Special Branch.

40:56.840 --> 40:59.600
He's also had a telephone conversation
with Jessie Eden,

40:59.680 --> 41:03.200
who is organising a committee
to resist wage cuts across the city,

41:03.280 --> 41:05.760
but he'll report more when he sees her.

41:05.840 --> 41:08.560
It's all in there, though.
Names and everything.

41:08.640 --> 41:11.280
I heard he had the Labour benches
roaring approval in the House today.

41:11.360 --> 41:12.280
Hmm.

41:12.360 --> 41:14.520
If only he meant it, he'd be dangerous.

41:15.040 --> 41:18.080
I didn't know you drank stout, Ada.
I thought it was gin.

41:18.560 --> 41:19.680
Times are hard.

41:22.200 --> 41:23.040
Hmm.

41:26.320 --> 41:28.080
Exactly how much did you lose?

41:29.240 --> 41:31.920
[sighs] Don't worry about it.
Tommy has a plan.

41:32.000 --> 41:36.000
He thinks that we can turn the collapse
of the stock exchange to our advantage.

41:36.080 --> 41:39.160
He says, "When others retreat,
you advance."

41:39.760 --> 41:42.800
He says we can wave cash
in the faces of desperate men.

41:45.000 --> 41:47.840
You tell your brother from me,
he's doing excellent work.

41:48.600 --> 41:53.320
I'm told two more military contracts for
vehicles and parts for the army in India

41:53.400 --> 41:54.640
-are on their way to him.
-Yeah.

42:03.120 --> 42:03.960
[Ada chuckles softly]

42:04.520 --> 42:05.360
Er...

42:09.920 --> 42:10.760
[exhales heavily]

42:11.760 --> 42:12.600
Fuck!

42:14.560 --> 42:15.400
[sighs]

42:18.840 --> 42:19.680
[knocking]

42:19.760 --> 42:20.600
[Tommy] Come.

42:20.680 --> 42:23.280
Mr Shelby, this arrived from New York.

42:23.360 --> 42:25.080
It's from Winston Churchill.

42:26.440 --> 42:29.560
He says he was in New York
and had dinner with Charlie Chaplin,

42:29.640 --> 42:32.040
and Chaplin mentioned your name.

42:32.120 --> 42:34.840
So, he's in New York,
and he doesn't mention the crash.

42:34.920 --> 42:37.600
No. Even though, in the bar last night,

42:37.680 --> 42:42.440
Mr Churchill's private secretary reckoned
he lost £75,000 in two hours.

42:44.640 --> 42:45.760
And you?

42:47.640 --> 42:50.000
Were you affected, Mr Shelby?

42:50.560 --> 42:53.720
I'm going to my apartment, Adam.
You lock up.

42:53.800 --> 42:56.840
Sorry, Mr Shelby,
you have one more appointment.

42:57.920 --> 43:00.160
You set it yourself.
It's in the diary from last week.

43:00.720 --> 43:02.120
A journalist from the Times.

43:02.200 --> 43:05.160
He used to be with the Birmingham Mail.
He sent you some questions.

43:05.760 --> 43:07.360
You said you wanted to meet him.

43:07.880 --> 43:08.880
He's outside.

43:09.880 --> 43:10.720
Yes.

43:11.880 --> 43:12.840
Send him in.

43:12.920 --> 43:13.760
[sighs]

43:17.280 --> 43:20.560
Er, Mr Shelby?
Mr Levitt from the London Times.

43:21.760 --> 43:22.760
Have a seat.

43:23.440 --> 43:25.320
Adam, you go home. I'll lock up.

43:31.120 --> 43:34.480
Mr Shelby, do you remember me?

43:36.680 --> 43:37.520
No.

43:38.480 --> 43:43.120
Once, in Small Heath, you were
burning photographs of the king.

43:43.720 --> 43:45.680
London Times. You've done well.

43:45.760 --> 43:47.000
As have you, sir.

43:47.560 --> 43:48.960
To put it mildly.

43:49.760 --> 43:52.360
I sent you a list of questions.

43:52.440 --> 43:54.000
Yeah, I misplaced them.

43:55.560 --> 43:57.840
-Yeah, well, you have a lot on your plate.
-Yes.

43:58.800 --> 44:01.720
All I remember is that
your questions interested me.

44:01.800 --> 44:02.640
Oh.

44:03.400 --> 44:04.360
Good.

44:05.680 --> 44:06.960
Sorry, this...

44:08.200 --> 44:09.480
Ah. Here we are.

44:10.080 --> 44:10.960
Yes, erm...

44:11.040 --> 44:11.880
[clears throat]

44:12.960 --> 44:14.200
Shall we, erm...

44:14.280 --> 44:18.760
So, Mr Shelby,
traditionally in this country,

44:18.840 --> 44:21.520
print journalists
take no interest in the, er...

44:21.600 --> 44:23.720
in the private lives of politicians.

44:24.280 --> 44:25.240
"Private lives"?

44:26.560 --> 44:29.640
But in these modern times,
especially in America,

44:30.400 --> 44:32.000
journalists are beginning to...
[stammering]

44:32.080 --> 44:34.920
Well, that is to say, yes, erm, shall I...

44:35.000 --> 44:37.720
So, readers are beginning to say...

44:37.800 --> 44:40.880
Want to know more about
the men who represent them.

44:41.400 --> 44:42.600
Of course.

44:42.680 --> 44:44.520
In these modern times.

44:45.520 --> 44:49.240
Whereas, before, it would have been
seen as ungentlemanly

44:49.320 --> 44:53.680
to ask a public figure questions about
personal matters or business affairs--

44:53.760 --> 44:55.640
Oh, well, no need to worry.

44:56.160 --> 44:57.640
I'm no gentleman.

45:01.560 --> 45:02.400
Hmm.

45:04.200 --> 45:06.040
Er, Mr Shelby, as I said in my letter,

45:06.120 --> 45:09.440
ten years ago, I was a journalist
on the Birmingham Evening Mail.

45:11.000 --> 45:15.400
And, of course, working in that city,
it was impossible not to know your name.

45:17.800 --> 45:20.560
And your reputation.

45:22.600 --> 45:23.440
[sighs]

45:23.520 --> 45:26.360
So, when I saw that you had been elected
as a Socialist--

45:26.440 --> 45:29.360
You reflected on the fact
that working people

45:29.440 --> 45:33.000
can indeed change their lives
for the better.

45:34.320 --> 45:38.640
Channel their abilities in new directions.
Discover better methods.

45:38.720 --> 45:41.200
Aim for happier outcomes.

45:41.280 --> 45:43.680
Even win awards for industry.

45:44.240 --> 45:46.040
You can write this down, Mr Levitt.

45:46.120 --> 45:49.040
The question I have for you,
Mr Shelby, is this.

45:51.200 --> 45:56.320
Was your conversion from bookmaker
to socialist politician

45:56.400 --> 45:59.520
a gradual thing, or...

46:00.360 --> 46:02.840
-a road to Damascus experience?
-[chuckles]

46:05.040 --> 46:06.040
Yeah.

46:07.760 --> 46:09.160
Now I hear that question.

46:10.720 --> 46:12.200
I remember receiving your letters,

46:12.280 --> 46:15.400
and I distinctly remember
your use of the word "bookmaker".

46:15.480 --> 46:18.920
Er... You...
Were you not a bookmaker, sir?

46:19.000 --> 46:23.920
Yes. I gained a licence in 1919
for on-track betting.

46:24.600 --> 46:27.160
But since 1923, I've made my fortune

46:27.240 --> 46:29.320
in the manufacture,
sale and export of motorcars.

46:29.400 --> 46:30.880
And lately, gin.

46:30.960 --> 46:33.840
And lately, three new homes
for orphaned children.

46:37.360 --> 46:39.120
You can write all this down, Mr Levitt.

46:40.640 --> 46:42.680
I have another question, Mr Shelby.

46:43.680 --> 46:46.920
In Birmingham,
at the time that I was there...

46:47.800 --> 46:50.000
there was a Major Campbell.

46:51.360 --> 46:52.720
He was found dead.

46:53.640 --> 46:56.360
A member of your family was charged.

46:59.880 --> 47:00.720
[chuckles]

47:02.840 --> 47:04.200
[sighs]

47:05.040 --> 47:07.960
All right, you answer me this.
Answer me this.

47:10.480 --> 47:12.960
What is your Tory newspaper
more afraid of?

47:13.960 --> 47:16.480
Is it evolution or revolution?

47:17.440 --> 47:20.480
And what is it about
working-class men like me,

47:20.560 --> 47:23.600
standing up in the House of Commons
and speaking from the heart?

47:24.280 --> 47:28.840
What is it that so troubles you
that you would try to undermine me?

47:28.920 --> 47:31.080
I am talking about specific events, sir.

47:31.160 --> 47:32.240
Which I don't recall.

47:33.720 --> 47:36.160
With which I was never personally linked.

47:36.240 --> 47:39.240
And after which,
all convictions were quashed.

47:46.640 --> 47:51.680
Yeah, now I... [smacks lips]
I recall receiving your letter.

47:53.120 --> 47:55.080
And I recall that when I did,

47:56.320 --> 48:00.320
I asked a colleague of mine
to carry out some research.

48:03.480 --> 48:04.680
Just a moment.

48:10.120 --> 48:11.280
Here we are.

48:21.480 --> 48:22.480
Ah.

48:22.560 --> 48:24.880
Michael Levitt, correct?

48:26.680 --> 48:27.920
-Yes.
-Yes.

48:28.680 --> 48:29.880
Journalist.

48:30.680 --> 48:31.800
Unmarried.

48:31.880 --> 48:33.680
Apartment in Maida Vale.

48:34.800 --> 48:38.680
An apartment opposite
the Underground station.

48:40.480 --> 48:42.840
"Unmarried" is underlined.

48:43.880 --> 48:46.840
"Mr Levitt enjoys walks in the park.

48:46.920 --> 48:48.160
Sometimes alone.

48:48.920 --> 48:50.560
Sometimes not alone.

48:52.040 --> 48:54.040
Sometimes with other men."

48:58.240 --> 48:59.080
[exhales heavily]

49:00.080 --> 49:04.200
"Sometimes with other men."

49:07.600 --> 49:10.560
I'm old-fashioned, Michael.

49:11.640 --> 49:14.600
I believe private lives
should remain private.

49:15.760 --> 49:17.880
Not everything modern is good, now, is it?

49:22.480 --> 49:27.080
You gonna write this down...
in your little fucking book?

49:34.440 --> 49:35.280
[door opens]

49:35.360 --> 49:36.280
[Polly] Well?

49:37.000 --> 49:39.640
Not yet. I didn't tell him.

49:40.640 --> 49:43.960
Him knowing or not knowing
has no relevance to what we are drinking.

49:44.040 --> 49:44.960
[Ada chuckles softly]

49:45.440 --> 49:46.560
It's vintage.

49:48.040 --> 49:49.480
-Fuck it.
-[chuckles]

49:51.720 --> 49:55.640
This... is for us.

49:57.920 --> 49:59.280
Let's drink to the baby.

50:02.280 --> 50:04.640
Polly, do you remember
when I was last pregnant?

50:06.520 --> 50:08.240
You were gonna take me to Cardiff.

50:09.000 --> 50:10.640
I was gonna get rid of it.

50:11.720 --> 50:13.160
What about this time?

50:16.760 --> 50:17.720
Imagine.

50:20.440 --> 50:21.680
The father's scared.

50:22.160 --> 50:23.400
He runs away.

50:24.120 --> 50:25.200
You're left.

50:25.280 --> 50:26.200
[Ada sighs]

50:27.320 --> 50:28.920
Oh, sweetheart.

50:29.720 --> 50:34.880
The baby's black, he's a bastard,
and it's Birmingham.

50:36.360 --> 50:37.960
But you don't care...

50:38.880 --> 50:41.480
because the world has changed.

50:42.720 --> 50:45.000
And the baby's eyes are golden.

50:45.560 --> 50:49.320
And you're gonna leave her with Aunt Polly
every day to look after.

50:49.880 --> 50:53.160
Aunt Polly who insisted
that you called her Elizabeth.

50:53.880 --> 50:55.360
How do you know it's a "her"?

50:56.280 --> 50:57.520
'Cause I'm Polly Gray.

50:59.080 --> 51:00.080
It's a girl?

51:00.800 --> 51:01.680
Golden.

51:01.760 --> 51:02.840
[chuckles]

51:02.920 --> 51:06.000
To be born in the year 1930,

51:06.080 --> 51:08.760
where everything will be changed
for the better.

51:10.720 --> 51:13.400
She'll be the colour of a Hollywood Oscar.

51:13.480 --> 51:14.320
[chuckles softly]

51:17.000 --> 51:18.240
What will Tommy say?

51:21.760 --> 51:22.960
Tommy knows.

51:25.120 --> 51:25.960
[chuckles]

51:26.720 --> 51:27.920
Hmm.

51:28.000 --> 51:32.880
He's also seen that the world's big
and round, and he says, "So what?"

51:37.720 --> 51:39.480
Tommy is right, you know, Ada.

51:41.200 --> 51:43.640
We are flying above the rules now.

51:44.680 --> 51:45.520
[chuckles]

51:46.720 --> 51:48.840
We're bold people, aren't we, Polly?

51:50.560 --> 51:51.840
That's the beauty.

52:06.280 --> 52:07.440
Hello, Cyril.

52:09.320 --> 52:10.920
At least you're still talking to me.

52:21.040 --> 52:22.120
Where is everyone?

52:22.200 --> 52:23.960
Your wife is upstairs with Ruby.

52:28.000 --> 52:29.640
You know, Frances,

52:31.320 --> 52:34.400
if there were to be a snap election
in this house today,

52:35.520 --> 52:36.560
wouldn't win it.

52:37.800 --> 52:40.960
Even if I were running
against the devil himself.

52:41.040 --> 52:41.880
Oh.

52:42.720 --> 52:44.360
-I gathered.
-Hmm.

52:45.840 --> 52:46.800
Where's Charles?

52:47.440 --> 52:49.000
Charles is in the stable.

52:50.920 --> 52:51.960
Thank you.

53:08.960 --> 53:09.800
[Tommy clears throat]

53:11.920 --> 53:13.520
Did Arthur explain?

53:13.600 --> 53:15.360
He said it's God's will.

53:17.320 --> 53:19.120
But you're not God!

53:29.760 --> 53:31.840
[sighs] No, I'm not God.

53:36.960 --> 53:37.920
Not yet.

54:01.560 --> 54:02.560
[footsteps approaching]

54:45.280 --> 54:47.280
[theme song playing]

55:01.560 --> 55:05.480
♪ On a gathering storm comes
A tall handsome man ♪

55:05.560 --> 55:10.080
♪ In a dusty black coat
With a red right hand ♪

55:17.840 --> 55:22.520
♪ A shadow is cast wherever he stands ♪

55:22.600 --> 55:26.680
♪ Stacks of green paper
In his red right hand ♪
